{"abstract":"The field of digital methods has emerged over the past two decades at the interface between media studies, science and technology studies (STS), computer science, and information design (Marres 2017; Rogers 2013). The core questions and ambitions covered closely resemble the kind of digital migration studies presented in this book. First, a commitment to studying wider sociocultural phenomena on, through, and with digital traces from online media. Second, a desire to critically engage with the role of these media, their platforms, and algorithms as infrastructures of social life. Third, a curiosity about how to wrongfoot entrenched divides between offline and online, actual and virtual, or qualitative and quantitative. And finally, ongoing experimentation with computational techniques for large-scale data capture analysis in conjunction with ethnographic approaches to deep sensemaking and interpretation. Indeed, to begin","author_count":5,"contributors":["Munk, Anders Kristian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-122725"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Big data","CARE","Critical proximity","Digital Methods","Gephi"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"caring as critical proximity a call for toolmaking in digital migration studies","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/handle/20.500.12657/51964/978-3-030-81226-3.pdf?sequence=1#page=243","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-122725","ids":["aau-fa31386a-382b-49e6-9f91-d62ae7f9254c"],"keyword":["Big data","Care","Critical proximity","Digital methods","Gephi","Navicrawler"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-3A39B0F6A627D68C04372A815BE49B69"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/handle/20.500.12657/51964/978-3-030-81226-3.pdf?sequence=1#page=243","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-81226-3_10","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning, The Techno-Anthropology Lab","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, AI for the People"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders Kristian","key":"munkpau","last":"Munk","locid":"1152f3e1-f7da-4ced-8a78-b16a32675dca","name":"Munk, Anders Kristian","orcid":"0000-0002-5542-3065","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Marie","key":"sandbergpedh","last":"Sandberg","name":"Sandberg, Marie","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Luca","key":"rossipedh","last":"Rossi","name":"Rossi, Luca","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Vasilis","key":"galispedh","last":"Galis","name":"Galis, Vasilis","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Martin Bak","key":"jørgensenpedh","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Martin Bak","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9783030812263"],"isbn":["9783030812256"],"pages":"235-245","publisher":"Palgrave Macmillan","status":"p","subtitle":"Caring For (Big) Data?","title":"Research Methodologies and Ethical Challenges in Digital Migration Studies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-fa31386a-382b-49e6-9f91-d62ae7f9254c"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Caring as Critical Proximity : A Call for Toolmaking in Digital Migration Studies","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-122725","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This chapter poses questions about research on a phenomenon that is polluted by politics, personal tragedies, ideological loyalties, and hazards for the subject of research. Is it possible to conduct research that contributes to freedom of movement, while reflecting on the emotions and political loyalties of the researcher? The chapter suggests four concrete principles for an emancipatory migration research paradigm and considers reflections, emotions, and narrations during and after the conduct of fieldwork at the Greek islands of Lesvos and Chios in the winter of 2019. This is also a call for future publications from researchers who have faced similar emotions, and dead ends in the field, and wish to engage in research compatible with solidarity with migrants.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384431"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Emotion","Migration research","Shame","Solidarity","Vulnerability","emancipatory"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the redundant researcher fieldwork solidarity and migration","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-81226-3_7","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384431","ids":["itu-d6fe373c-39d0-4617-bcd0-37c338ba929e"],"keyword":["emancipatory","emotion","migration research","shame","solidarity","vulnerability"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-413B1F8D1715BEE58E35E65D8539E004"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-81226-3_7","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"167-194","publisher":"Palgrave Macmillan","series":"Approaches To Social Inequality And Difference","status":"p","subtitle":"Caring For (Big) Data?","title":"Research Methodologies and Ethical Challenges in Digital Migration Studies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d6fe373c-39d0-4617-bcd0-37c338ba929e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Redundant Researcher : Fieldwork, Solidarity, and Migration","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384431","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Activeness is a key concept in smart grid visions, but little is known of what this activeness entails. By combing the literature on marketisation/ performativity with critical consumption studies, we outline our findings from a case study of a smart grid project in Sweden. Using a mixed method methodology, we critically approach various tensions, doubts and frictions that occur in the process of constructing ‘activeness.’ For decades, the design of the Swedish energy system has been guided by assumptions that users base their actions on what is profitable and thus behave as calculative agents, and we found this assumption also acted as a guiding principle in this project. However, we encountered project employees who continuously pondered the appropriateness of configuring the smart grid around an economic cornerstone, and they hesitated when trying to explain how their configuration was aligned with the notion of activeness. By describing their scripted users as ‘passively active’ and ‘actively active,’ they seemed to cling to the notion of activeness while simultaneously stretching the actual meaning of this word. We conclude that these ambiguities in the configuration of the smart grid do not contribute to any sense of collective rule or environmentally friendly solutions.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Wallsten, Anna","Galis, Vasilis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383921"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Linköping University"],"keyword":["Activeness","Consumption","Economisation","Smart grid","Users"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the discreet charm of activeness the vain construction of efficient smart grid users","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84375356/The_discreet_charms_of_activeness_May2019_final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383921","identifier":[{"id":"85068744504","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b5e69f70-9d1e-4bec-bbef-ab1f1fe9526f"],"keyword":["Activeness","consumption","economisation","smart grid","users"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B733663131BEF162DEEC55230B947BCE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84375356/The_discreet_charms_of_activeness_May2019_final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"linköping university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Linköping University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna","key":"wallstenpau","last":"Wallsten","name":"Wallsten, Anna","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/17530350.2019.1639067","issn":["17530350","17530369"],"issn_org":["17530350"],"journal":"Journal of Cultural Economy","pages":"1-20","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"Journal of Cultural Economy"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85068744504"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b5e69f70-9d1e-4bec-bbef-ab1f1fe9526f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The discreet charm of activeness : the vain construction of efficient smart grid users","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383921","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"This chapter aims to discuss the binary thinking that echoes the Cartesian split, which creates divisions between the body and mind, society and the individual, technology and biology, disability and impairment, thereby proliferating an infected, ambivalent and hybrid approach to the human subject. The study of dis/ability and research in the field of technoscience have followed relatively parallel paths in the history of ideas. The social model of disability has played an important role as a scientific and political instrument for identifying and confronting structural and legal barriers that impede the full participation and citizenship of people with disabilities, linking social theory to activism. The chapter summarises conceptualisations of dis/ability through the analytical lenses of science and technology studies, which remove the focus from the individual and turn it to relations and inextricable combinations of subjects and objects. Aside from the market, the other pillar of modernity, the sovereign state, uses biopolitical mechanisms to regulate life in organised societies.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381622"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Binary Thinking","Biopolitics","Cartesian Split","Social Model of Disability","Technoscience"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"we have never been able bodied thoughts on dis ability and subjectivity from science and technology studies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-381622","ids":["itu-177051fa-d773-4940-9ee0-eaf7f8f282ce"],"keyword":["Binary Thinking","Biopolitics","Cartesian Split","Social Model of Disability","Technoscience"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E851AC5305D38267662D22BA7F4253E7"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"edition":"2nd","eisbn":["9780429430817"],"pages":"404-418","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"Routledge Handbook of Disability Studies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-177051fa-d773-4940-9ee0-eaf7f8f282ce"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"We have never been able-bodied : Thoughts on dis/ability and subjectivity from science and technology studies","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381622","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Public spaces are often contested sites involving the political use of socio-material arrangements to check, control and filter the flow of people. In Sweden, the recently established police project (REVA) in is an attempt to strengthen ‘internal border’ controls. This paper discusses the emergence of practices in which activist groups organized and performed resistance through the use of counter technologies in the transport sector. We explain how a hybrid alliance of human and nonhuman others generated new virtual and urban spaces and provided temporary autonomous zones, to groups of undocumented immigrants. REVA Spotter, for example, was a tool, a manifesto and a peaceful means of resistance to the REVA policing methods through continuous Facebook status updates on identity checks at metro stations in Stockholm. The technology enabled reports on location and time of ticket controls to warn travellers in real time. Attempts by authorities to exert control over the ‘spatial’ underground were thereby circumvented by the effective development of an alternative infrastructural ‘underground’ consisting of assemblages of technologies, activists, undocumented immigrants, texts and emails, smart phones and computers. Based on ‘netnographic observations’ and interviews, the paper utilizes the case of the REVA to illustrate processes and practices that simultaneously configure the powerful surveyor, the discriminated and those who contest these politics through hybridities of cyber/material, human/nonhuman and urban/virtual space. The paper argues that by configuring such hybrid alliances, activists provided cyber-material autonomy to undocumented immigrants and other travellers in the metro, thereby creating new virtual and urban spaces for mobility and flows.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis","Summerton, Jane"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382601"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","The Swedish National Road and Transport Research Institute (VTI)"],"keyword":["REVA","Social media","cyber material autonomy","undocumented immigrants"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"we are all foreigners in an analogue world cyber material alliances in contesting immigration control in stockholm s metro system","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83025971/GalisSummerton.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382601","identifier":[{"id":"85032196599","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-59f0726a-0617-4a2d-b19d-ef40c10d37f1"],"keyword":["REVA","Social media","cyber-material autonomy","undocumented immigrants"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AC6E070B089B0042EE8535E56BB8DF5D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83025971/GalisSummerton.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"the swedish national road and transport research institute","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"The Swedish National Road and Transport Research Institute (VTI)","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jane","key":"summertonpau","last":"Summerton","name":"Summerton, Jane","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/14742837.2017.1383892","issn":["14742829","14742837"],"issn_org":["14742837"],"journal":"Social Movement Studies","pages":"1-19","paperid":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/14742837.2017.1383892","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"Social Movement Studies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85032196599"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-59f0726a-0617-4a2d-b19d-ef40c10d37f1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"We are all foreigners in an analogue world : Cyber-material alliances in contesting immigration control in Stockholm’s metro system","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382601","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"This article examines current appropriations of social media by activists of the radical left in Greece and Sweden. Previous research has shown that the discourse concerning social media’s empowering potential is embedded in commercial values that contradict the value systems of many activists who engage in struggles against the current economic system. We employ the notion of détournement, which describes how social movements turn something aside from its normal course or purpose. Based on interviews and online ethnographic observations, we seek to understand how and with what consequences social media facilitate and limit collective action. The article enhances our understanding of activists’ social media use by turning our attention to the sociotechnical impact of social media on collective action initiated by leftist groups as well as the relationship between ideological loyalties and the political economy of corporate social media.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis","Neumayer, Christina"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383696"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Alternative media","Digital activism","Détournement","Indymedia","REVA"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"laying claim to social media by activists a cyber material détournement","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/2056305116664360","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383696","identifier":[{"id":"85028630741","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-a64f4d20-afdb-46de-b2a7-c2d39d59f0c9"],"keyword":["Indymedia","REVA","alternative media","digital activism","détournement"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8758F536807D5C9E2BA9403AE0A65096"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/2056305116664360","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82527373/SocialMediaSociety_2016_Galis_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, TiP and Games Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christina","key":"neumayerpau","last":"Neumayer","name":"Neumayer, Christina","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/2056305116664360","issn":["20563051"],"issue":"3","journal":"Social Media + Society","pages":"1-14","paperid":"2056305116664360","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Social Media + Society","volume":"2"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85028630741"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a64f4d20-afdb-46de-b2a7-c2d39d59f0c9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Laying Claim to Social Media by Activists : A Cyber-Material Détournement","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383696","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"This article considers media narratives that suggest that hiding in trucks, buses, and other vehicles to cross borders has, in fact, been a common practice in the context of migration to, and within, Europe. We aim to problematize how the tension between the materiality of bordering practices and human migrants generates a dis/abled subject. In this context, dis/ability may be a cause or consequence of migration, both in physical/material (the folding of bodies in the crypt) and cultural/semiotic terms, and may become a barrier to accessing protection, to entering and/or crossing a country, and to performing mobility in general. Dis/ability and migration have not been associated in the literature. We adopt an analytical symmetry between humans and non-humans, in this case between bodies and crypts. By suggesting an infected, ambivalent, and hybrid approach to the human subject, the body-crypt traveling border challenges the essentialist dichotomies between technology and biology, disability and impairment. The articles and reports upon which we rely were collected through extensive searches of databases/archives of online newspapers and news websites.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis","Tzokas, Spyros","Tympas, Aristotle"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381739"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Greece"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Department of European Civilization Studies, Hellenic Open University","National and Kapodistrian University of Athens"],"keyword":["Border crossing","Dis ability","Migration","Narratives","crypts"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"bodies folded in migrant crypts dis ability and the material culture of border crossing","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/soc6020010","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381739","identifier":[{"id":"85020077013","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-1fd992f0-91e0-45be-99e5-113c09d2abf6"],"keyword":["border-crossing","crypts","dis/ability","migration","narratives"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A731EBD1182109839AC33F3E365210BA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82348426/societies_06_00010.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/soc6020010","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, TiP and Games Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gr","key":"department of european civilization studies hellenic open university","loc":["Greece","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Department of European Civilization Studies, Hellenic Open University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gr","key":"national and kapodistrian university of athens","loc":["Greece","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"National and Kapodistrian University of Athens","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Spyros","key":"tzokaspau","last":"Tzokas","name":"Tzokas, Spyros","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Aristotle","key":"tympaspau","last":"Tympas","name":"Tympas, Aristotle","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/soc6020010","issn":["20754698"],"issue":"2","journal":"Societies","publisher":"MDPI","status":"p","title":"Societies","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85020077013"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1fd992f0-91e0-45be-99e5-113c09d2abf6"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Bodies Folded in Migrant Crypts : Dis/Ability and the Material Culture of Border-Crossing","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381739","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Infrastructures are habitually associated with enabling, with facilitating mobility. Attention to accidents and related failures of infrastructures, due to accidental or endemic reasons, has substantially enriched the historiography of infrastructures while, at the same time, pointing to limits to mobility. What we still lack is an adequate number of studies that link infrastructures to structural immobility, to systematic disabling. As we see it, the history of the co-construction of infrastructures and disability can help us to raise critical questions regarding both infrastructures and disability. We expect this to be beneficial to both historians of technology (and other historians of infrastructures) and scholars who specialize in critical disability studies. The paper will present ongoing research on a whole range of infrastructures (from ICT to biotechnology) that are embedded in transport and related infrastructures. For the purpose of the ‘New Directions in the History of Infrastructures’ conference, we will focus on two representative case studies: (1) Attempts to construct migration control transport-related infrastructures at the EU borders. The emphasis here is on infrastructures as shaped by the clash of the technologies used by those who have tried to block access to Europe and those who have tried to enter Europe. We will pay special attention to the infrastructures tried at the Greek borders over the last 25 years, when attempts to block mass migration flows from Eastern Europe and Asia came to challenge the rhetoric of globalization. (2) Plans to introduce and operate urban transport infrastructures that produced new versions of disability and/or reconfigured versions of existing disability. We aim to show the gradual defeat and dis/abling of the ‘undesired urban subject’ through the implementation and configuration of control, surveillance and policing infrastructures. While our first case traces attempts at infrastructure-crossing in the land and sea borders of Greece (e.g. in the Evros area in north-east Greece and in the Aegean sea), our second follows the history of surveillance and policing infrastructures of so-called ‘internal border control’, that is control in urban settings (e.g. the Athens and the Stockholm metro). As our argument goes, transportation infrastructures (and the other technological infrastructures connected to them) have tended to constitute boundaries of protected identities and privileges, an institutionalized filter for ‘undesirable’ bodies. Similarly, transport infrastructures were sites of contestations and struggles over “who belongs and who doesn’t” - who is allowed to move freely in national or urban spaces and who isn’t. Transport infrastructures became sites for regulating and controlling certain groups. Seen like this, transport technologies, at remote national borders and in the heart of a national metropolis, were a key field for sociotechnical battles that produced dis/abled-displaced bodies, that is, a new corporeal subject. Dis/abled-displaced human bodies were the other side of enabling border/urban transport technologies. Through studying the history of the formation of specific border and urban transport infrastructures we aim to study the deformation of citizenship and the displacement of the clandestine, that is, the amputation of identity (cultural-national, corporeal). Our theoretical argument is that citizenship and/or dis/ability do not reside solely in the body or in society. They are an effect that comes into being when certain embodiments “are irreducibly caught up in a web of constitutive connections that disturb the very idea of the human being “(Shildrick, 2010).","author_count":3,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis","Tympas, Aristotle","Tzokas, Spyros"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382196"],"event":{"end":"2014-09-28","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2014-09-26","title":"New Directions in the History of Infrastructure","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Greece"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Department of European Civilization Studies, Hellenic Open University","National and Kapodistrian University of Athens"],"keyword":["Critical Disability Studies","Disability","Infrastructure studies"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"integrating critical disability studies into the historiography of infrastructures","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.ptt-museum.dk/conference_2014/conference_programme_/","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382196","ids":["itu-3ec142c4-24a0-4565-8316-c53805d2b7c2"],"keyword":["critical disability studies","disability","infrastructure studies"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-CA9282CD0F950F0A7E4EAA9F8A9912F7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.ptt-museum.dk/conference_2014/conference_programme_/","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, Interdisciplinary Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gr","key":"national and kapodistrian university of athens","loc":["Greece","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"National and Kapodistrian University of Athens","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gr","key":"department of european civilization studies hellenic open university","loc":["Greece","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Department of European Civilization Studies, Hellenic Open University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Aristotle","key":"tympaspau","last":"Tympas","name":"Tympas, Aristotle","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Spyros","key":"tzokaspau","last":"Tzokas","name":"Tzokas, Spyros","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3ec142c4-24a0-4565-8316-c53805d2b7c2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Integrating Critical Disability Studies into the Historiography of Infrastructures","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382196","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"In light of the financial crisis and harsher migration policies, actions initiated by radical civil society groups are on the rise. This project argues that in this time it is important to understand the reclaiming of online media by civil society. To reach this understanding we will explore how activists of the radical left have mobilized protests and solidarity actions through online media. We have chosen to locate our study in Sweden and Greece. Greece has found itself in the eye of the global financial storm whereas Sweden is coming out of the recession more favorably. In general, there is an excitement around civil society uses of online media leading to a wave of studies in this area. With our project, we underscore that such uses of online media also create potential frictions with reference to two integral dimensions of these media: cyber-material aspects of online media as well as the oxymoron of radical activists on the left using capitalist media platforms and how this influences their political identity. Therefore, there is a need to understand the cyber-material impact of online media on collective action initiated by civil society as well as the relationship between ideological loyalties and the political economy of mainstream online media. How and with what consequences does cyber-materiality facilitate as well as limit collective action? Does the use of commercial digital platforms by Swedish and Greek activists radicalize these media or is there a risk for radical collective action of becoming recuperated by commercial online media?","author_count":2,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis","Neumayer, Christina"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384972"],"event":{"end":"2014-09-19","place":"Torun, pl","start":"2014-09-17","title":"European Association for the Study of Science and Technology Conference 2014","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Collective action","Digital platforms","Financial crisis","Migration policies","Online media","Political economy","Political identity","Swedish and Greek activists","cyber materiality","radical civil society"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the reclaiming of online media by civil society greece amp sweden","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://easst.net/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/easst2014.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384972","ids":["itu-fbc026ac-d449-4c35-9d04-00a9ca6c75d8"],"keyword":["Swedish and Greek activists","collective action","cyber-materiality","digital platforms","financial crisis","migration policies","online media","political economy","political identity","radical civil society"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-5988456EC6A548C5272556173E82A4A3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://easst.net/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/easst2014.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, Interdisciplinary Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christina","key":"neumayerpau","last":"Neumayer","name":"Neumayer, Christina","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-fbc026ac-d449-4c35-9d04-00a9ca6c75d8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The reclaiming of online media by civil society : Greece &amp; Sweden","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384972","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"The process of translation has both an excluding and including character. The analysis of actor networks, the process of mobilizing alliances, and constructing networks is a common and worthwhile focus. However, the simultaneous betrayals, dissidences, and controversies are often only implied in network construction stories. We aim to nuance the construction aspect of actor–network theory (ANT) by shining the analytical searchlight elsewhere, where the theoretical tools of ANT have not yet systematically ventured. We argue that we need to understand every process of translation in relation to its simultaneous process of treason, and to add antonyms for Callon’s problematization, intressement, enrollment, and mobilization. This enables us to describe powerlessness not as a state but as a process. Our case focuses on the network building around measures for disabled people in the construction of the Athens Metro, during the period 1991-1993. The discussion highlights the efforts of disability organizations to intervene in the initial construction works of the metro project and the simultaneous actions of the Greek government to exclude disability organizations from the design process and to disrupt the accessibility-metro actor network.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis","Lee, Francis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384390"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["justice inequality protest politics power governance space place scale dynamics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a sociology of treason the construction of weakness","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84890513785","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384390","identifier":[{"id":"84890513785","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d45c51b5-746f-42cf-aada-4168fd2a6012"],"keyword":["justice inequality protest politics power governance space/place/scale dynamics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-327611C99FB49398AC734DAAF30310E8"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, Interdisciplinary Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Francis","key":"leepau","last":"Lee","name":"Lee, Francis","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["01622439","15528251"],"issn_org":["01622439"],"issue":"1","journal":"Science, Technology & Human Values","pages":"154-179","paperid":"1","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Science, Technology & Human Values","volume":"39"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d45c51b5-746f-42cf-aada-4168fd2a6012"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Sociology of Treason : The Construction of Weakness","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384390","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Against what the dominant rhetoric of globalization would led us to assume, we live in a world that is overwhelmed by the presence of borders. Erasing some borders, so as to allow for easier and increased flow of commodities, was frequently coupled by the introduction of additional borders, to disable the free flow of people. Similarly, the loosening of borders within a cluster of partner states (like the EU ones) has frequently led to the introduction of even more rigorous borders between these states and their exterior. Whatever the benefits of globalization might have been, they were not all that apparent to the mass of migrants who have been risking their lives to cross borders. Delineating and demarcating borders has always involved the use of material arrangements to check, control and filter the flow of people. In regards to the EU borders of the recent years, a suggestive body of literature has focused on their reinforcement through the deployment of all kinds of state-of-the-art policing technology, from ICT to biotech. Our research makes a notice of this literature but seeks to approach the technological borders of the EU through the study, also, of the technology that migrants used to try to make it to Europe. A good part of our broader research project concerns a specific group of migrants, namely migrants with dis/abilities. Dis/ability may be a cause or consequence of migration and may become a barrier to both accessing protection and to entering a country. In any case, migrants with dis/abilities have remained largely invisible. Moreover, very little is known about cross-border mobility of dis/abled migrants. In the framework of our research, dis/ability is not solely treated as bodily disadvantage or social oppression. We treat dis/ability as an interaction between impaired displaced bodies and disabling socio-material barriers. In this sense, dis/ability cannot be detached from the performative role of policing technology. Instead, however, of staying at something like the technology of a patrolling EU police helicopter with electronic eyes, we want to study how this state-of-the-art EU helicopter was actually used against a humble migrant rubber raft. Preliminary research has shown to us that this technological battle has also generated a new form of human body. This type of body is defined by different sorts of disablements and displacements. Dis/abled-displaced human bodies are the other side of enabling border technologies. Based on the stories that we have found, our research suggests that technological battles over border production have also produced clandestine dis/ability. In this paper we present a sample of our broader research by introducing to three versions of the technological clash between migrants and EU/Greek border authorities: truck crypts used by migrants versus thermal cameras used by border authorities to detect them, landmines and fences that border authorities could rely on to limit migrant flows, and rubber boats and related artifacts used by migrants at sea against superior EU and Greek detection and ‘push-back’ technology.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis","Tzokas, Spyros","Tympas, Aristotle"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384170"],"event":{"end":"2013-09-21","place":"Paris, fr","start":"2013-09-19","title":"Tensions of Europe","year":"2013"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Greece"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Department of European Civilization Studies, Hellenic Open University","National and Kapodistrian University of Athens"],"keyword":["Mobility","battles","cladestine"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on dis abled clandestine bodies producing european borders through uneven technological battles","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-384170","ids":["itu-c5eb7cc2-982a-428c-b075-9fc0dbe275cf"],"keyword":["battles","cladestine","mobility"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-44A651A650F326EC21D554004A631F52"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gr","key":"department of european civilization studies hellenic open university","loc":["Greece","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Department of European Civilization Studies, Hellenic Open University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gr","key":"national and kapodistrian university of athens","loc":["Greece","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"National and Kapodistrian University of Athens","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Spyros","key":"tzokaspau","last":"Tzokas","name":"Tzokas, Spyros","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Aristotle","key":"tympaspau","last":"Tympas","name":"Tympas, Aristotle","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["00000000"],"journal":"CoRR","status":"p","title":"CoRR","title_org":"Tensions of Europe"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-c5eb7cc2-982a-428c-b075-9fc0dbe275cf"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On dis/abled clandestine bodies : Producing European borders through uneven technological battles","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384170","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Public spaces are often contested sites involving the political use of sociomaterial arrangements to check, control and filter the flow of people (see Virilio 1977, 1996). Such arrangements can include configurations of state-of-the-art policing technologies for delineating and demarcating borders of various kinds, as well as for identifying and displacing undesired individuals/groups/bodies. A case in point is a recently-established police project (REVA) in Sweden for strengthening the so-called internal border control. Specifically, several underground stations in Stockholm now have checkpoints where policemen are entitled to ask citizens to identify themselves. However, authorities imposing and defining borders do not only create boundaries to fence the insecurity of a homogeneous community. Borders are also made to be crossed. The crossing of borders often involves a complicated set of ritual practices, symbolic gestures and movements (Stavrides 2001). This paper will discuss the emergence and implications of these practices, as well as the ways in which activist groups organize and perform resistance through the use of counter technologies (ICT and other information technologies) in the transport sector. We will show how the use of alternative technologies by activist groups provides temporary spatial asylum to groups of immigrants without papers. REVA Spotter, for example, is a tool, a manifesto and a peaceful mean of resistance to the REVA policing methods through continuous Facebook status updates on identity checks at the metro stations in Stockholm and reports on locations and time of ticket controls for warning travelers. Thus the attempts by authorities to exert control over the (spatial) arena of the underground is circumvented by the effective developing of an alternative infrastructural \"underground\" consisting of assemblages of technologies, activists, immigrants without papers, texts and emails, homes, smart phones and computers. Investigating the embedded politics of contested spatial arrangements as characteristic of specific societies one can discover not only the uses and meanings of space but also the politics of creating and sustaining different sociomaterial subjects (Stavrides 2001). Thus in this paper, resistance cannot be detached from the (counter-)performative role of (policing) technology. One tentative observation is that struggles between border-enforcing technologies and human migrants have generated a new form of human subject that is defined by various sorts of displacements. In attempts at political control within transport systems, displaced human subjects are the other side of enabling technologies.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis","Summerton, Jane"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-385012"],"event":{"end":"2013-10-12","place":"San Diego, us","start":"2013-10-09","title":"Society for Social Studies of Science","year":"2013"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","The Swedish National Road and Transport Research Institute (VTI)"],"keyword":["Activist resistance","Border control","Counter technologies","Public spaces","Sociomaterial arrangements"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"underground politics transport networks urban borders bodies and assemblages of resistance","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-385012","ids":["itu-ff33c9e0-887c-4cbf-9218-59328c7ba237"],"keyword":["Activist resistance","Border control","Counter technologies","Public spaces","Sociomaterial arrangements"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-5F06C7801709C50ABF3A85FD34146CD9"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"the swedish national road and transport research institute","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"The Swedish National Road and Transport Research Institute (VTI)","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jane","key":"summertonpau","last":"Summerton","name":"Summerton, Jane","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ff33c9e0-887c-4cbf-9218-59328c7ba237"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Underground Politics : Transport Networks, Urban Borders/Bodies and Assemblages of Resistance","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-385012","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Delineating and, more so, demarcating borders, has always involved the use of material arrangements to check, control and filter the flow of people. In regards to the EU borders, a suggestive body of literature has focused on the deployment of all kinds of state-of-the-art policing technology. Our paper makes a notice of this literature but moves on to discuss the co-production of technological borders and migrants with dis/abilities. In this case, dis/ability may be a cause or consequence of migration, and may become a barrier to both accessing protection and to entering a country. Nonetheless, migrants with dis/abilities have remained largely invisible and very little is known about their cross-border mobility. We do not solely treat dis/ability as a bodily disadvantage or social oppression, but as an effect of somatechnics, that is, clandestine dis/ability is always bound up with a variety of policing technologies, repressive techniques and military technics. Instead, however, of staying at the technology of a fast patrolling boat with electronic eyes, we move on to show how exactly this EU electronic boat has clashed with a humble migrant floating device. We are interested in the somatechnological battle over border production by focusing on the creation of dis/abled-displaced bodies. Our research aims to show that the battle between border-producing technologies and human migrants also generates a new corporeal subject. Dis/abled-displaced human bodies are the other side of enabling border technologies. Based on the stories that we have found at local and national newspapers in Greece, we focus on how somatechnological battles over border production have produced clandestine dis/ability.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis","Tzokas, Spyros","Tympas, Aristotle"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381974"],"event":{"end":"2013-06-19","place":"Linköping, se","start":"2013-06-17","title":"Missing Links","year":"2013"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Greece"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Department of European Civilization Studies, Hellenic Open University","National and Kapodistrian University of Athens"],"keyword":["Borders","Dis ability","Migration","Policing Technology","Somatechnics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the production of clandestine dis abled bodies european borders migrants and policing technics","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-381974","ids":["itu-300067f7-c229-44e5-af14-5aabf6900a92"],"keyword":["Borders","Dis/ability","Migration","Policing Technology","Somatechnics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-F93672456F5584B917B4FB6DC811D80C"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gr","key":"department of european civilization studies hellenic open university","loc":["Greece","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Department of European Civilization Studies, Hellenic Open University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gr","key":"national and kapodistrian university of athens","loc":["Greece","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"National and Kapodistrian University of Athens","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Spyros","key":"tzokaspau","last":"Tzokas","name":"Tzokas, Spyros","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Aristotle","key":"tympaspau","last":"Tympas","name":"Tympas, Aristotle","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-300067f7-c229-44e5-af14-5aabf6900a92"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On the production of clandestine dis/abled bodies : European borders, migrants and policing technics","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381974","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Several academic traditions have addressed epistemological objectivity and/or partisanship in the study of technoscientific controversies. On the one hand, positivist and relativist scholars agree that the political commitments of the social researcher should not impinge on scientific enquiry, while on the other hand, feminist and Marxist scholars not only take stands in diverse technoscientific debates, but even claim their agendas to be more credible than those of orthodox scientists. Such perspectives stress that all research is partisan in one way or another because it involves questions of who controls, manipulates, and establishes decisions, facts, and knowledge. With this in mind, it is possible to identify different forms of partisan research including capture by participants, de facto and overt partisanship, and mercenary scholarship. These different forms of partisan scholarship are characterised by differences in the motives underlying epistemological choices of research topic and method, personal commitments to the fields studied, use of research findings in controversies, and positioning of results in wider debates. Two examples help to illustrate partisan scholarship: first, a study of new technologies for managing climate change (carbon dioxide capture and storage); and second, the construction of the new underground metro system in Athens and its accommodation of accessibility standards. Both cases entail partisan positions and raise similar concerns about the orthodox epistemological assumptions underpinning sociotechnical systems, especially when it comes to technoscientific controversies. Supporting STS partisan scholarship, therefore, enables greater social and democratic engagement with technoscientific development.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis","Hansson, Anders"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383687"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Linköping University"],"keyword":["Partisan STS scholarship situated epistemology technoscientific controversy Carbon Capture Storage CCS disability accessibility Athens metro"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"partisan scholarship in technoscientific controversies reflections on research experience","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84866008220","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383687","identifier":[{"id":"84866008220","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-a5c27000-dbea-4833-b10a-9a80b6f6d0d8"],"keyword":["Partisan STS scholarship, situated epistemology, technoscientific controversy, Carbon Capture Storage (CCS), disability, accessibility, Athens metro"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-DB93073C707770C9C94B1E7E5F76A546"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"linköping university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Linköping University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"hanssonpau","last":"Hansson","name":"Hansson, Anders","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["09505431","14701189"],"issn_org":["09505431"],"issue":"3","journal":"Science as Culture","pages":"335-364","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"Science as Culture","volume":"21"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a5c27000-dbea-4833-b10a-9a80b6f6d0d8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Partisan Scholarship in Technoscientific Controversies : Reflections on Research Experience","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383687","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
